versions
---
Zofia: Which one makes them search for Len's papers?
Morwenna: This one. The replacement keeps the application form and takes out the search duty. I nearly put it on top.
Mara: They've offered me time for the Bill if I accept that replacement.
Zofia: He could already send a letter.
Mara: Yes. At second reading we argued about the Bill's main principles. A duty to answer, I said. Now my own party's organisers want the duty gone before committee.
Hugh: You may lose their support if you refuse. But keeping the form without the search would leave Len exactly where he was.
Mara: Then I go back and refuse.
Morwenna: Shall I cancel tomorrow's campaign meeting? You wrote “agreement secured” in the invitation.
Mara: Give me the list. I'll ring them myself.

Mara: The Lords added reasons and an appeal. Now they want to know who pays the staff to do it.
Hugh: They should know. A search duty requires people to search.
Mara: I'll take the costing back to the organisers. With the appeal still in it.
Morwenna: Keep this copy, please. If either House changes it, they still have to agree the same text. I have run out of colours for the tabs.
Kit: I could bring some from rehearsals.
Morwenna: Do they say “actually agreed”?
Kit: Mostly “stand here”.

len_answer
---
Zofia: The signature. It's the unit-account custodian's this time. Not another letter saying somebody will consider the policy.
Ollie: And the decision date matches the published attachment. I checked, which sounds obvious until you've brought the wrong one on two buses.
Zofia: Len, this is the answer about the purpose in the account. I'm going to read it.
Zofia: “The description of the operation as solely neutral intervention is corrected. The secret Sèvres plan between Britain, France and Israel preceded the fighting: Israel would attack Egypt, followed by an Anglo-French ultimatum and intervention.”
Ollie: Here's the evidence list. The plan was made on 22 to 24 October 1956. Israel invaded Sinai on 29 October. Britain and France issued the ultimatum on 30 October and began bombing on 31 October.
Zofia: “The original wording is retained beside this annotation. The sequence and agreement do not support the account's claim of a solely neutral purpose.”
Ollie: There's the address he kept giving you. On the application. They copied it right down to—
Zofia: Ollie?
Ollie: Nothing. I was leaving room. You used to look up here and then tell me what he'd said.
Zofia: I know.
Ollie: Right. So the custodian made the search because of the new duty, and issued the answer. The published account links to the evidence. I'll put the publication copy in the file.
Zofia: I wanted him to interrupt that bit. He'd have made you say who was taking the letter.
Ollie: You don't have to tell me what he would have said.
Zofia: No. Sorry.
Ollie: Do you want me to stay while you read the rest?
Zofia: Yes. Don't leave the umbrella there. You'll forget it.
Ollie: I haven't forgiven it yet. It's coming with me.

correction
---
Ruth: Ewa, publication copy. Check your name and the evidence attachment before I close the envelope.
Ewa: My name is there. My signed rota is there. The first allegation is still above it.
Ruth: Yes. My signed correction is beside it. I carried the rehearsal shift into the theft account. You had finished work before the transfer. The released log and your evidence show that.
Banda: Ewa gave you that signed rota before you corrected the date. Put that in. She had finished her shift; I logged the contractor coming through afterwards.
Ruth: Her credit remains. So does my responsibility. I concealed the loss. That protected me.
Ewa: I gave you the route and the completed shift. You used the wrong day. Leave that plain.
Ruth: It is plain in the signed text.
Grace: But why do we still do this? Another envelope, another arrangement, after they already proved it?
Banda: Next doorkeeper opens the transfer log, there's Ewa's name under suspicion. Put her rota and your correction there. Same page. I won't have the next shift stopping her again.
Grace: And who pays for Ewa to come and check it? Because the next reader isn't missing her shift.
Ewa: This visit has an agreed paid hour. I asked before I came.
Ruth: This publication used the House's separate review arrangement. It did not use the new Act's government-account power.
Zofia: So the family scheme can start again?
Ruth: No. The sponsored access arrangement has ended. The correction does not renew it.
Banda: Ollie used to wait for me by that door. He can't now. I still wouldn't keep quiet about the wrong shift to get him back in.
Ewa: My name has been cleared. That does not put every lost thing back.
Hugh: Vale's mother's contribution is acknowledged in the separate review too. The evidence supports her help in the rescue. It does not establish that she acted alone.
Zofia: Is he going to accept it?
Hugh: I cannot answer for him. His conduct and his mother's contribution remain separate matters.
Mara: Alec's page stays in the campaign file. I checked it this morning.
Hugh: “Not established” remains beside the successful applications. My preservation rule must cover a refusal that hurts us as well as a correction we sought.
Ewa: The envelope is checked. I have twenty minutes left. Let us not spend them congratulating the envelope.

hale_answer
---
Morwenna: This is the custodian's signed decision, with the evidence and reasons attached. I checked the published copy too. You can read it sitting down, if you want.
Zofia: He used to object when I sat on the folder.
Morwenna: I can hold it.
Zofia: No. I've got it. Hale, the Office has answered your application. The Act required the search and the decision. This isn't the old policy hold.
Zofia: “The case summary is annotated to record conscience as the applicant's stated reason for refusal. Refusal is not evidence of an admission of disloyalty. The summary's inference of such an admission is retracted.”
Morwenna: The original stays visible with the annotation. That's the next paragraph.
Zofia: I saw. I was going to skip it.
Morwenna: Please don't. We argued for that part too.
Zofia: “The original summary is preserved with this decision and the supporting evidence. The request to reverse the historical settlement is refused. Annotation records the reason given; it cannot restore the displaced order or undo the applicant's death.”
Morwenna: There's a review route if you think they failed to answer the actual demand. It doesn't mean we have to use it.
Zofia: They did answer it.
Morwenna: Yes.
Zofia: You can ask me.
Morwenna: Ask you what?
Zofia: What he said. You keep waiting for me to tell you.
Morwenna: I was waiting for you. I don't know what else to do with my face.
Zofia: Me neither.
Morwenna: We can stay a little longer. Or go. I won't decide that for you.
Zofia: One minute. Then I want something to eat. I haven't had lunch.
Morwenna: I brought two rolls. Unless they're flat now. The folder was on them.
Zofia: Flat is fine.

bank_then
---
Ewa: Zofia. Will you sit here with me?
Zofia: Yes. Here. I'm sitting here with you.
Ewa: Your sleeve is caught. Let me move it.
Zofia: Say that again.
Ewa: Your sleeve. Under my hand.
Zofia: I heard it.
Ewa: Can you hear me now?
Zofia: Yes.
Grace: Zofia?
Zofia: Yes. Grace. I can hear you.
Grace: Don't just say yes because you can see I'm asking.
Zofia: You said don't just say yes.
Grace: Okay.
Zofia: I saw you at the door. I knew you wanted me. I kept waiting for him instead.
Grace: I know. I was there.
Zofia: I don't know how to make that less horrible.
Grace: You could answer me when I ask if you need your bag.
Zofia: I need my bag. Please.
Ewa: Grace carried it here. You can leave it on the bench for a minute.
Zofia: Mum, I can't tell you all of it yet.
Ewa: Then tell me whether you are cold.
Zofia: Yes.
Ewa: Your coat is here. Put your arms through.

bank_now
---
Ewa: Morwenna sent a message. She says you both ate the rolls. She seemed worried I would think she hadn't fed you.
Zofia: They were extremely flat rolls.
Ewa: Eaten is the important part.
Zofia: At the memorial, after the answer, she waited. I got cross with her for waiting. She hadn't even done anything.
Ewa: Did you tell her that?
Zofia: On the way back. Badly. I said she could stop looking at me like that. Then I said sorry.
Ewa: And what happened?
Zofia: She gave me the other roll.
Ewa: You have told me what she did. What happened to you?
Zofia: I thought getting the words right would feel different. I kept checking them. As if there was a bit I'd missed that would let me hear him.
Ewa: Did you find one?
Zofia: No.
Ewa: You nearly drowned. You were hearing things I could not hear. I was frightened before anyone showed me a wrong date on a form.
Zofia: I know. I used to think you were saying I hadn't seen anything.
Ewa: Tell me which part you saw.
Zofia: I saw you waiting on this bench. That day. Before I could hear you, I could see you waiting. I wanted to answer, and I didn't know if my voice would sound right because I couldn't hear it either. I said yes anyway.
Ewa: I heard you.
Zofia: That's the part I want to start with.
Ewa: You can tell me on the way. Can you come home with me now?
Zofia: Yes. I'll carry the bag.
